<div class="Section1">

<h1>TCS Project — August Review &amp; Upcoming Work Plan</h1>
<p class="subtitle"><strong>Sukesh P (M24IM1007)</strong> — M.Tech Joint IDRP, IIT Jodhpur &amp; AIIMS Jodhpur &middot; Project No. R/S/TCS/AA/20250112</p>
<p class="subtitle">Prepared for the 2:30 PM work-plan discussion with Prof. Ajay Agarwal &middot; 18 September 2026</p>
<p class="subtitle">Built strictly from documents in your project folder (dated Aug 2025 &ndash; Aug 2026, evidence weighted toward August 2026). Nothing below is invented; unresolved items are marked as such.</p>

<hr>

<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>The project (SERS-based detection of sepsis biomarkers NAM, LTA, LPS) has a strong, largely finished <strong>reference/computational foundation</strong>: DFT + powder Raman characterization of all three biomarkers is complete to varying degrees of rigor (NAM strongest, LPS moderate, LTA weakest/needs rerun). The <strong>Ag/Si SERS substrate</strong> reliably enhances the benchmark dye R6G (confirmed across multiple independent studies, including a very strong August 12 result reaching SNR&nbsp;450), but <strong>no biomarker (NAM, LTA, or LPS) has yet been successfully, reproducibly detected by SERS</strong>. An LTA signal that looked positive on 14 August was later shown, on pixel-level re-examination, to be a single-detector-pixel artifact — corrected to NOT DETECTED. LPS has never been attempted on any substrate. As of today (18 September), <strong>no experimental work has been executed since the mid-August planning documents</strong> — the immediately-next experiment (LPS detection, fully protocoled) has not yet started. The project's own most recent self-assessment (dated 3 Aug 2026) describes the platform as "a promising research prototype and reference-analysis framework, not yet a clinically validated sepsis diagnostic device," and that framing still holds. This document reconstructs what was actually done in August, what August's own documents said should happen next, and turns that into a concrete plan to discuss with Prof. Ajay today.</p>

