<h2>5. Results Obtained</h2>
<h3>NAM</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reference powder spectrum from 22 independent measurements, mean pairwise correlation 0.986.</li>
<li>41 reproducible bands (400-1800&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;), 30 matched to DFT modes, MAE 9.27&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, RMSE 10.29&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, r=0.9996.</li>
<li>Laser-damage threshold: safe up to 80% power/25&nbsp;s and 70-90%/30&nbsp;s; destroyed at 90%/60&nbsp;s.</li>
<li>Two novel vibrational assignments: 872&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; = hydroxymethyl wagging (not ring mode); 731&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; = carboxylic-acid O&ndash;H wag (47.9% displacement, 0.5% ring character) &mdash; argues <em>against</em> muramic acid as the source of the widely-used bacterial 730&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; SERS discrimination band.</li>
<li>On-substrate (SERS) detection: mean SNR only <strong>0.89</strong> in the June/July branch; on the fresh 12&nbsp;Aug substrate, no reproducible hit above SNR&nbsp;3 in any of 3 etch conditions &times; 2 power levels.</li>
<li>NAM MW corrected from 275.25 to <strong>293.27&nbsp;g/mol</strong> (13 Aug) &mdash; old molarity figures ~6.5% too high.</li>
</ul>
<h3>LTA</h3>
<ul>
<li>12-spot reproducibility study: 7 characteristic peaks, average correlation 0.9787, average peak CV 16.74%.</li>
<li>Expanded 136-file branch: best condition 20&nbsp;s/40% power, SNR&nbsp;139.03, but an unresolved NaN-valued fitting branch exists.</li>
<li>14&nbsp;Aug SERS attempt: apparent 1095&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; hit (SNR&nbsp;7-9) &mdash; <strong>reclassified NOT DETECTED</strong> after pixel-level analysis showed a single-pixel detector artifact.</li>
</ul>
<h3>LPS</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fragment DFT (KDO, heptose, D-glucosamine, myristic acid, phosphoric acid) + NNLS fit vs. powder Raman: R&sup2;=0.1799, RMSE 0.185, Pearson r=0.5078 (21% R&sup2; improvement over equal-weighting baseline). Explicitly characterized in-source as "a preliminary reconstruction rather than a high-fidelity identity proof."</li>
<li>No SERS/substrate measurement has ever been attempted for LPS.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Substrate / R6G</h3>
<ul>
<li>June batch: 84.58% peak-matching accuracy, mean shift error +0.81&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, MAE 3.25, RMSE 3.67&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, mean reproducibility correlation 0.7754 (below 0.90 target).</li>
<li>12&nbsp;Aug batch: all 8 standard R6G bands detected on all 3 etch-time substrates; 2-min etch strongest (SNR&nbsp;450 at 1509&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;); etch-time effect confirmed <strong>non-monotonic</strong> (10-min beats 5-min but not 2-min).</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Failed / Null / Inconclusive Experiments</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Experiment</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Root cause (as documented)</th></tr>
<tr><td>Substrate reproduction, July 2026</td><td>Failed/null</td><td>Contamination during etching/rinsing/drying (exact mechanism not pinned down); corrective actions (fresh reagents per batch, documented cleaning protocol, immediate blank-after-fabrication) defined but only partially verifiable as followed in the 12&nbsp;Aug batch</td></tr>
<tr><td>NAM-on-substrate detection (June/July branch and 12&nbsp;Aug batch)</td><td>Null (not reproducible)</td><td>Ranked root-cause analysis: (1) most likely &mdash; NAM lacks a thiol/strong Ag-binding group and has no resonance at 785&nbsp;nm (weak adsorption + weak intrinsic scattering, both at once); (2) droplet spreading on hydrophilic black-Si; (3) possibly-too-low effective near-surface concentration; substrate-inactivity and instrument-miscalibration explicitly excluded as causes</td></tr>
<tr><td>LTA-on-substrate detection, 14&nbsp;Aug</td><td>Initially apparent positive, corrected to null</td><td>Single-detector-pixel fixed-pattern artifact at pixel 640, not a molecular signal; a genuine band on this instrument spans 4-5 pixels</td></tr>
<tr><td>LTA fragment-model fit (expanded 136-file branch)</td><td>Inconclusive</td><td>NaN-valued weights/fit metrics in one analysis branch &mdash; explicitly flagged as "not usable until rerun/verified"</td></tr>
<tr><td>LPS fragment-model global fit</td><td>Inconclusive/modest</td><td>R&sup2;=0.18 &mdash; peak-region matches reasonable, whole-spectrum shape agreement weak</td></tr>
<tr><td>NAM full-spectrum shape agreement (vs. DFT)</td><td>Inconclusive despite good peak-position match</td><td>Cosine similarity only 0.5868, Pearson 0.1710 on the 81-spectrum validation branch &mdash; "should not be summarized as a perfect overlay"</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7. Major Findings</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Calibration offset:</strong> instrument reads &minus;2 to &minus;3&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; low vs. the textbook Si TO phonon (517.8 vs. 520-521&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;) &mdash; a fixed, not random, offset. &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; tolerance now applied project-wide.</li>
<li><strong>Artifact-rejection rule:</strong> any candidate peak narrower than 3 detector pixels (~5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;) must be rejected &mdash; discovered via the LTA pixel-640 false positive, now mandatory before any peak call across all analytes.</li>
<li><strong>SNR vs. integration time is superlinear</strong> on this instrument (5.9&times; actual gain vs. 2.2&times; shot-noise-predicted, 5&rarr;25&nbsp;s) &mdash; longer integration is an unusually effective, currently under-used lever.</li>
<li><strong>NAM molecular weight correction</strong> (275.25&rarr;293.27&nbsp;g/mol) affects all prior NAM molarity figures by ~6.5%.</li>
<li><strong>R6G etch-time response is non-monotonic</strong> (2&nbsp;min &gt; 10&nbsp;min &gt; 5&nbsp;min) &mdash; substrate optimization cannot assume a simple trend, and only one substrate was made per condition so far (no batch-to-batch reproducibility data exists).</li>
<li><strong>Novel NAM vibrational assignment (872&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;)</strong> and <strong>evidence against a muramic-acid origin for the disputed bacterial 730&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; band</strong> &mdash; potentially the project's most citable scientific contribution to date, independent of the SERS-detection outcome.</li>
<li><strong>Root-cause consensus on NAM's SERS difficulty:</strong> structural (no Ag-binding group, non-resonant at 785&nbsp;nm), not an instrument or substrate-activity problem &mdash; substrate and instrument are both independently confirmed functional via R6G and Si-reference measurements respectively.</li>
</ul>

