<p class="key">Numerical acceptance criteria above (SNR&ge;3, &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, etc.) are already used consistently across the August protocol documents, but <strong>have not been explicitly signed off by Prof. Ajay</strong> &mdash; recommend confirming these are the agreed thresholds before they're used to justify any published claim.</p>

<h2>15. Experimental Strategy</h2>
<p>Sequential, not parallel, by design intent in the source documents: LPS first (best precedent, de-risks the whole platform), then NAM (optimization track, already well-characterized as structurally hard), then LTA (repeat of a corrupted result). Each experiment uses a same-session blank and an R6G go/no-go gate before committing biomarker material, and every peak call now requires the despike + &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; + 3-pixel-width criteria established from the 13-14&nbsp;Aug findings.</p>

<h2>16. Validation Strategy</h2>
<p>As detailed in Phase 3 above: same-session blanks, R6G activity gate, &ge;3 spots/condition minimum (9 for primary concentrations), negative controls (unetched Si for LPS), and &mdash; the biggest currently-missing piece &mdash; independent-substrate replicates, which have never been run for any condition in this project to date.</p>

<h2>17. Data Analysis Plan</h2>
<p>As detailed in Phase 4 above: despike &rarr; ALS baseline &rarr; Savitzky-Golay &rarr; normalize &rarr; peak detection (SNR&ge;3, &ge;3px, &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;) &rarr; per-spot evaluation before averaging &rarr; (once positive) dose-response/LOD statistics &rarr; (much later) classification.</p>

<h2>18. Risks and Contingency Plans</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Risk</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Impact</th><th>Mitigation</th><th>Backup plan</th></tr>
<tr><td>Weak/no SERS signal for NAM even after AgNO&#8323; optimization</td><td>Root-cause analysis identifies a structural (not fixable-by-nanostructure) limitation</td><td>High &mdash; NAM is the most novel target</td><td>Move to capture chemistry (MPBA)</td><td>Treat NAM as spectral-marker-only via bacterial capture, not a free-molecule binding target</td></tr>
<tr><td>Substrate batch-to-batch variability</td><td>Only 1 substrate/condition ever made; etch-time response already shown non-monotonic</td><td>Medium-high &mdash; undermines any reproducibility claim</td><td>Add independent-substrate replicates to every future experiment</td><td>None documented &mdash; genuinely open</td></tr>
<tr><td>Detector fixed-pattern artifacts mistaken for signal</td><td>14&nbsp;Aug LTA pixel-640 case</td><td>High if undetected &mdash; false positive risk</td><td>Mandatory despiking + 3-pixel-width rule, now standard</td><td>N/A, already implemented going forward</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coffee-ring/droplet spreading on hydrophilic black-Si</td><td>Identified as a contributing factor to NAM/LTA null results</td><td>Medium</td><td>Hydrophobic barrier rings, 2&nbsp;&micro;L confined drops</td><td>Raman mapping instead of single-point (suggested in NAM_SERS_Sensor_Report, not yet implemented)</td></tr>
<tr><td>LPS acute toxicity (H300)</td><td>Confirmed hazard classification, Sigma L2630</td><td>High (safety)</td><td>Fume hood weighing, locked storage, toxic-waste disposal</td><td>N/A &mdash; strict protocol adherence required</td></tr>
<tr><td>HF handling across all three experiments</td><td>Standard across every fabrication step</td><td>High (safety)</td><td>Double gloves, face shield, calcium gluconate gel on hand, never work alone</td><td>N/A &mdash; non-negotiable</td></tr>
<tr><td>LTA solution instability</td><td>Stable only 2 days at 4&deg;C aqueous</td><td>Medium &mdash; may partly explain the 14&nbsp;Aug result</td><td>Prepare fresh or single-use frozen aliquots</td><td>Discard and re-prep if age uncertain</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insufficient instrument characterization (mW, HF concentration)</td><td>Flagged repeatedly across nearly every August document, never resolved</td><td>Medium &mdash; undermines comparability to literature</td><td>One-time measurements, quick to resolve</td><td>N/A</td></tr>
<tr><td>TCS deliverable scope mismatch</td><td>No document defines the exact TCS requirement</td><td>High &mdash; could invalidate current prioritization</td><td>Confirm scope with Prof. Ajay today</td><td>Proceed with current biomarker-detection roadmap as the default until told otherwise</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>19. Expected Deliverables</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Near-term (this plan):</strong> LPS/NAM/LTA experimental verdicts with full logs; resolved laser-power/HF-concentration gaps; NAM manuscript submission.</li>
<li><strong>Medium-term:</strong> first reproducible biomarker SERS detection; LOD/dose-response data for that biomarker; resolved LTA NaN-branch and NAM DFT-generation harmonization.</li>
<li><strong>Longer-term (scope-dependent, needs TCS confirmation):</strong> multi-biomarker panel, ML classification layer, clinical/blood-matrix validation, possible POC hardware &mdash; none of which are yet scoped against an actual TCS requirement in any document reviewed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>20. Questions for Prof. Ajay</h2>
<div class="qbox">
<ol>
<li><strong>What is the exact TCS deliverable/requirement?</strong> (No document defines this precisely &mdash; project number R/S/TCS/AA/20250112 is the only concrete TCS reference found.)</li>
<li>What is the expected project deadline / milestone schedule? (Not established from available documents.)</li>
<li>Given LPS/NAM/LTA are all still at "not yet detected" or "just corrected from a false positive," which should be the priority if time is constrained &mdash; continue as sequenced (LPS&rarr;NAM&rarr;LTA) or reprioritize?</li>
<li>What validation level does TCS expect at this stage &mdash; proof of concept, working prototype, or something closer to deployable?</li>
<li>Are FESEM/EDS/UV-Vis characterization (specified in the original Masters-report methodology but never evidenced as done) actually required, or can the project proceed on Raman/SERS evidence alone?</li>
<li>Should the NAM manuscript be finalized/submitted in parallel with the experimental work, or does experimental work take priority right now?</li>
<li>Is there a hardware/software integration expectation from TCS, or is the current scope purely the sensing chemistry?</li>
<li>What resources (reagents like HNO&#8323;/H&#8322;O&#8322;, instrument time, possible commercial reference substrates) can be approved/expedited?</li>
<li>How should responsibility be divided if other lab members (e.g., Dr. Mohamed Zaki Hafeez) are also working on adjacent parts of this project?</li>
<li>Should the numerical acceptance criteria used throughout the August protocols (SNR&ge;3, &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, etc.) be treated as formally agreed, or does Prof. Ajay want different thresholds?</li>
</ol>
</div>

<h2>21. Decisions Required in Today's Meeting</h2>
<div class="checklist">
[ ] Confirm project objective (as it relates to the TCS deliverable specifically)<br>
[ ] Confirm final deliverable (POC / prototype / deployable system)<br>
[ ] Confirm timeline / deadline<br>
